  "title": "An attributes file left by setup changes the bytes the reset restores",
  "file": "src/evals/runner.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Suppose a case repository's base-commit setup commands leave an untracked .gitattributes behind (for example one that sets a working-tree encoding or line endings) and also change a tracked file. The reset that is supposed to restore the committed bytes then writes that file back in the converted form. The reviewer reads different content from the case, yet preparation reports success.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "cd $TMPDIR/f1r && git init -q && printf new > CURRENT.txt && git add . && git commit -qm c; echo 'CURRENT.txt working-tree-encoding=UTF-16LE' > .gitattributes; printf changed > CURRENT.txt; git reset --hard -q HEAD; xxd CURRENT.txt; printf changed > CURRENT.txt; git --attr-source=HEAD reset --hard -q HEAD; xxd CURRENT.txt",
    "output": "git 2.54.0. Plain reset: 00000000: 6e00 6500 7700 (n.e.w. as UTF-16LE). With --attr-source=HEAD: 00000000: 6e65 77 (new). If setup leaves the tracked file untouched, the plain reset prints 'failed to encode' but the file keeps the committed bytes 'new'."
   },
   "evidence": "Head runner.ts:691 runs `await worktreeGit([\"git\", \"reset\", \"--hard\", \"-q\", head], { cwd, signal })` right after `prepareEvalGates(..., true)` executes the setup commands. worktreeGit (src/git/command.ts:125-129) adds `--attr-source=<empty tree>` only when the subcommand is `diff` or `log`. For `reset` it sets only core.attributesFile=/dev/null and blanks filter drivers, so built-in attributes such as working-tree-encoding and eol in an untracked working-tree .gitattributes still apply on checkout. This code is new in base..head. Severity is minor: setup comes from the trusted base commit, and it has to both write such an attributes file and modify a tracked file. The cost is a skewed eval trial, not production state."
